@page "/docs/common/downloads"

@* Downloads: every way IzzyBlazor hands the user a file, in one place. A DataGrid's export links to
   endpoints you map (DataExport's Excel and CSV writers, DataExport.Read for any other format, a
   PDF recipe with QuestPDF), a chart's Download (a PNG drawn in the browser, chart-export.ts), a
   chart's PNG posted in a form for a PDF the server builds, and printing. The PDF endpoint was
   under Data from an API's "Export files" until Nov 10, 2026. *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="downloads">
    <Lead>
        Endpoints you map serve a grid's rows as Excel, CSV or PDF, and the browser saves a chart as a PNG. To put a chart
        in a PDF, send its picture to your server in a form.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">A grid's rows</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>ExportExcelHref</Code> and <Code>ExportCsvHref</Code> put an Export button on a DataGrid that links to an
            endpoint you map. The link carries the grid's <Code>filter</Code>, <Code>sort</Code>, <Code>search</Code> and
            <Code>columns</Code>, and <Code>DataExport.WriteExcelAsync</Code> and <Code>WriteCsvAsync</Code> write those rows,
            streamed as they're read:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs (server)">@Excel</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>The file has the rows the user sees, in every page, with the columns shown in their order.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>It's a link, so it works on static pages and with JavaScript off.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                The fields you give <Code>DataExport</Code> decide what can be written, whatever the URL asks for. See
                <Anchor Href="docs/components/data-grid/export">DataGrid export</Anchor>.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Other formats</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            For a format the library doesn't write, <Code>DataExport.Read</Code> gives the columns and rows the Excel and CSV
            writers would get, and you write the file. This PDF endpoint uses
            <Anchor Href="https://www.questpdf.com" External>QuestPDF</Anchor>; any PDF library works the same way, and the
            grid lists it with <Code>&lt;DataGridExportFormat Label="Export PDF" Href="/orders/export.pdf" /&gt;</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs (server)" HighlightLines="10,11">@Pdf</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>A PDF is laid out whole, so this one reads every row first. Cap the rows, as above, where a filter can match millions.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                The query runs when the rows are read. An endpoint that streams the file, as CSV does, reads the first row
                before it starts the response, so a query that fails can still answer with an error.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>Each column's <Code>Key</Code> names it in a keyed format such as JSON, and its <Code>Text</Code> writes a value as CSV does.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>QuestPDF is free under its Community license for organizations below $1M in revenue, and paid above it.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                For rows read by <Code>DynamicColumn</Code>, build the fields with <Code>DataFields.ByName</Code>, by the same
                names and types, so the keys and values match the grid's:
                <Code>DataFields.ByName&lt;IDictionary&lt;string, object?&gt;&gt;("Margin %", typeof(double))</Code>.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">A chart's picture</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>Download</Code> puts a button by a chart's title that saves it as a PNG named after the title. A button of
            your own does the same with <Code>data-ib-chart-download</Code> set to the chart's <Code>id</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@ChartButtons</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The browser draws the picture, at twice the chart's size on screen and in the color scheme the page is in. It works
            on static pages too, and with JavaScript off there's no picture. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/chart/download">Chart download</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">A chart in a PDF</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            To put a chart in a PDF your server builds, send its picture in a form. A file input with
            <Code>data-ib-chart-png</Code> set to the chart's <Code>id</Code> gets the chart's PNG as the form is sent, and
            the endpoint reads it like any uploaded file:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Revenue.razor" HighlightLines="3">@ChartForm</Code>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs (server)" HighlightLines="3,16">@ChartPdf</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>The form is sent as <Code>multipart/form-data</Code>, which a file needs, whatever its <Code>enctype</Code> says.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>The form's other fields go with it, such as a date range or a note for the report. For several charts, add an input for each.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Leave out <Code>data-enhance</Code>: the answer is a file, not a page.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>With JavaScript off, or a chart that couldn't be drawn, the input is empty and the endpoint gets no file. This one answers with an error; it could build the PDF without the chart.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Printing</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The browser's Print prints the page, and its Save as PDF makes a PDF with no code on the server. Charts and tables
            print as they show.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Excel = """
        app.MapGet("/orders/export.xlsx", (HttpContext context, ShopDb db) => Results.Stream(
            stream => DataExport.WriteExcelAsync(stream, db.Orders.AsNoTracking(), context.Request.QueryString.Value,
                DataFields.Of<Order>(), "Orders", cancellationToken: context.RequestAborted),
            "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet", "orders.xlsx"));
        """;

    private const string Pdf = """
        using QuestPDF.Fluent;
        using QuestPDF.Infrastructure;

        QuestPDF.Settings.License = LicenseType.Community;

        app.MapGet("/orders/export.pdf", async (HttpContext context, ShopDb db) =>
        {
            var orders = db.Orders.AsNoTracking()
                .Select(o => new OrderRow { Id = o.Id, Customer = o.Customer.Name, Total = o.Total });
            var export = DataExport.Read(orders, context.Request.QueryString.Value,
                DataFields.Of<OrderRow>(), context.RequestAborted);
            var rows = new List<object?[]>();
            await foreach (var row in export.Rows)
            {
                if (rows.Count == 5_000)
                {
                    return Results.Problem("Narrow the filter: a PDF holds at most 5,000 orders.", statusCode: 413);
                }

                rows.Add(row);
            }

            var pdf = Document.Create(document => document.Page(page =>
            {
                page.Margin(36);
                page.Content().Table(table =>
                {
                    table.ColumnsDefinition(columns => export.Columns.ToList().ForEach(_ => columns.RelativeColumn()));
                    table.Header(header => export.Columns.ToList().ForEach(c => header.Cell().Text(c.Caption).Bold()));
                    foreach (var row in rows)
                    {
                        for (var i = 0; i < row.Length; i++)
                        {
                            table.Cell().PaddingVertical(2).Text(export.Columns[i].Text(row[i]));
                        }
                    }
                });
            })).GeneratePdf();
            return Results.File(pdf, "application/pdf", "orders.pdf");
        });
        """;

    private const string ChartButtons = """
        <Chart Items="quarters" Argument="q => q.Quarter" Title="Revenue by region, 2026" Download>
            ...
        </Chart>

        <Button StartIcon="Lucide.Download" data-ib-chart-download="signups">Download</Button>
        <Chart id="signups" Items="days" Argument="d => d.Day" Title="New signups, last 7 days">
            ...
        </Chart>
        """;

    private const string ChartForm = """
        <form method="post" action="/reports/revenue.pdf">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <input type="file" name="chart" data-ib-chart-png="revenue" hidden />
            <Button Submit StartIcon="Lucide.FileDown">Download PDF</Button>
        </form>

        <Chart id="revenue" Items="quarters" Argument="q => q.Quarter" Title="Revenue by region, 2026">
            ...
        </Chart>
        """;

    private const string ChartPdf = """
        using QuestPDF.Fluent;

        app.MapPost("/reports/revenue.pdf", async (IFormFile? chart) =>
        {
            if (chart is null)
            {
                return Results.BadRequest("The chart's picture didn't come with the form.");
            }

            using var image = new MemoryStream();
            await chart.CopyToAsync(image);
            var pdf = Document.Create(document => document.Page(page =>
            {
                page.Margin(36);
                page.Header().Text("Revenue report, 2026").FontSize(18).Bold();
                page.Content().PaddingTop(12).Image(image.ToArray());
            })).GeneratePdf();
            return Results.File(pdf, "application/pdf", "revenue.pdf");
        });
        """;
}
